  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "Isaac has written down one integer two times and another integer three times. The sum of the five numbers is \\(100,\\) and one of the numbers is \\(28.\\) What is the other number?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\( 8 \\)",
    "B": "\\( 11 \\)",
    "C": "\\( 14 \\)",
    "D": "\\( 15 \\)",
    "E": "\\( 18 \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The number \\(28\\) must be the integer written either twice or three times, so these two cases cover every possibility.\n\nIf \\(28\\) is written twice, its contribution is \\(2\\cdot28=56\\). The remaining three equal integers must sum to \\(100-56=44\\). This is impossible because \\(44=3\\cdot14+2\\) is not divisible by \\(3\\).\n\nTherefore \\(28\\) is written three times. Its contribution is \\(3\\cdot28=84\\), leaving \\(100-84=16\\) for two copies of the other integer. That integer is\n\\[\n16\\div2=\\boxed{8}.\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(3\\cdot28+2\\cdot8=100\\). The answer is choice A.",
